-- title:
--   Decomposition-group equivariance of the pinned function-field embedding
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ is `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`, $A_2$ is a valuation subring of $\bar{\mathbb Q}$, $C := A_2.\mathrm{valuation.Completion}$ is the completion of $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ for the associated valuation, $F_2 := \mathtt{ratClosure}\,A_2$ is the topological closure of the prime subfield inside $C$, and
--   $$\mathcal X_C := \mathcal X_0 \times_{\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O_0} \operatorname{Spec} C,$$
--   the fibre product of $f_0 : \mathcal X_0 \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O_0$ with $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the composite ring map $\mathcal O_0 \to F_2 \to C$.
--
--   **Numerical data.** $N$ is a non-zero squarefree natural number, $q$ and $q'$ are primes with $q' \neq q$, $5 \le q$, $5 \le q'$, and neither dividing $N$; $D$ is a non-zero natural number divisible by $2Nqq'$. The valuation subring $A_2$ satisfies $A_2.\mathtt{LiesOverPrime}\ q$, i.e. $q$ lies in the non-units of $A_2$, and `A₂.DecompositionIsometric ℚ` holds, i.e. the valuation of $A_2$ is preserved by every element of the decomposition subgroup of $A_2$ over $\mathbb Q$. Finally $v_2$ is a height-one prime of $\mathcal O_{\mathbb Q}$ containing $q$.
--
--   **The definite quaternion algebra and its orders.** Rationals $a_1, b_1$ satisfy `IsDefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt q'`: $a_1 < 0$, $b_1 < 0$, and for every height-one prime $v$ the completion $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1] \otimes_{\mathbb Q} \mathbb Q_v$ is a division algebra exactly when $q \in v$ is replaced by $q' \in v$. Here $\Lambda_1$ is a maximal order (an order maximal among the orders containing it), $R_1$ is an Eichler order of level $N$ (an intersection of two maximal orders of relative index $N$) with $R_1 \le \Lambda_1$, and $n_1$ is a finite-idelic unit lying in `primeHeckeSet R₁ q`. The further hypotheses on this side are: $\mathtt{meetOrder}\,R_1\,n_1 = R_1 \cap n_1 R_1 n_1^{-1}$ is an Eichler order of level $Nq$ (`hS₁`); conjugation of that order by $n_1$ returns it (`hnorm₁`); the double shift by $n_1$ on the class set of its finite-idelic stabiliser is the identity (`hsq₁`); the two class sets involved are finite; and `hlaws₁` asserts `ClassSetHeckeLaws N q Λ₁ R₁ n₁`, namely (four clauses, summarised here) that the edge Hecke matrices pairwise commute, that the vertex Hecke matrices pairwise commute, that the two degeneracy push-forwards intertwine edge and vertex Hecke operators at primes other than $q$, and that the common kernel of the two push-forwards is stable under every edge Hecke operator.
--
--   **The indefinite quaternion algebra and the moduli data over $\mathbb Z[1/D]$.** Rationals $a, b$ satisfy `IsIndefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt a b q q'`: $0 < a$ or $0 < b$, and $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b] \otimes_{\mathbb Q} \mathbb Q_v$ is a division algebra exactly at the places containing $q$ or $q'$; $\Lambda$ is a maximal order in it. $\bar F$ (`Fbar`) is a field over $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ which is a curve over $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ in the sense of `IsCurveOver` (principal divisors of degree zero exist, residue fields of places are finite over $\bar{\mathbb Q}$, and $\Omega_{\bar F/\bar{\mathbb Q}}$ is free of rank one) and is essentially of finite type over $\bar{\mathbb Q}$. A scheme $X$ with a morphism $\pi_X$ to $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the localisation of $\mathbb Z$ away from $D$ is given, together with a $\bar{\mathbb Q}$-point $\bar s$ of that base and an assignment `pt` sending a fake elliptic curve with $\Lambda$-action and $N$-level structure over a commutative ring $S$, together with a base point $s$, to a morphism to $X$ over $s$; `pt_iso`, `pt_pullback`, `pt_surjective` and `pt_injective` are the four coarse-moduli clauses for `pt` (invariance under isomorphism, compatibility with base change along ring maps, and bijectivity on points with values in an algebraically closed field, modulo isomorphism), the universal property not being assumed.
--
--   **The curve model, the Galois action and the Hecke tower.** $\mathfrak M$ is a `CurveModel` of $\bar F$ over $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ (a proper smooth integral curve $\mathfrak M.C$ over $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ with a chosen isomorphism $\mathfrak M.\mathtt{ffEquiv} : \bar F \cong$ the function field of $\mathfrak M.C$, and a bijection between closed points and places), $e_{\mathfrak M} : \mathfrak M.C \to X \times_{\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z[1/D]} \operatorname{Spec}\bar{\mathbb Q}$ is an isomorphism whose composite with the second projection is the structure morphism $\mathfrak M.\mathtt{toBase}$, and `gal` is a homomorphism from $\operatorname{Aut}(\bar{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ to the group of semilinear automorphisms of $\bar F$ over $\bar{\mathbb Q}$. Further, $\mathbb T$ is a Hecke tower `TowerData` over $\bar F$ for the pair $(q,q')$, `galT` are the semilinear Galois actions on its layers, $W$ and `WT` are prescribed semilinear automorphisms of $\bar F$ and of the layers, and `tw` is a `ModuliTowerWitnessD` for $\Lambda, N, q, q', D, \bar F, X, \pi_X, \bar s, \mathtt{pt}, \mathfrak M, e_{\mathfrak M}, \mathtt{gal}, \mathbb T, \mathtt{galT}, W, \mathtt{WT}$, i.e. the witness relating places of the tower to fake elliptic curves with level data and recording the Galois and Atkin–Lehner compatibilities of that dictionary.
--
--   **Local data at $q$.** $\iota_2$ is an injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra map of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ into $2 \times 2$ matrices over $F_2$, and $\rho_2$ is the induced homomorphism of unit groups into $\mathrm{PGL}_2(F_2)$ (`hρ₂`). $\varpi_2$ is a pseudo-uniformizer of $F_2$ relative to $C$ whose image in $C$ is $q$ (`hϖ₂`), and the holomorphic ring `HolRingOf ϖ₂ ρ₂` is a domain. The families $s_2$ and $\mathtt{sf}_2$, indexed by the primes $\ell \notin \{q,q'\}$, consist of units of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ and of its finite adelisation, and `hs₂` requires for each $\ell$ that the component of $\mathtt{sf}_2(\ell)$ at each place not containing $q$ is $s_2(\ell) \otimes 1$, that its components at the places containing $q$ are $1$, that the product of the diagonal image of $\ell$ with $\mathtt{sf}_2(\ell)^{-1}$ lies in `levelHeckeUSet Λ₁ (meetOrder R₁ n₁) ℓ` when $\ell \mid N$ and in `primeHeckeSet (meetOrder R₁ n₁) ℓ` otherwise, and that the reduced norm of $s_2(\ell)$ is $\ell$. The groups $\Gamma_2$ are prescribed by `hΓ₂0`, which characterises $\Gamma_2(\mathrm{none})$ as the set of units lying in `CosetGraph.awayUnits R₁ v₂` whose reduced norm has even $q$-adic valuation, and by `hΓ₂ℓ`, which sets $\Gamma_2(\ell)$ to be the intersection of $\Gamma_2(\mathrm{none})$ with its conjugate by $s_2(\ell)$. The elements $w_2$ and $\bar w_2$ are Atkin–Lehner-type units: `hw₂` requires $w_2(\mathrm{none})$ to lie in `awayUnits R₁ v₂` with reduced norm $q$, and $w_2(\ell)$ to lie in `awayUnits (meetOrder R₁ (sf₂ ℓ)) v₂` with reduced norm $q$; `hwbar₂` requires (two analogous blocks of clauses, for $\mathrm{none}$ and for each $\ell$) that the reduced norm of $\bar w_2$ be $q'$, that its local image be a unit of the local box of $R_1$, respectively of `meetOrder R₁ (sf₂ ℓ)`, at each place other than $v_2$ not containing $q'$, and that conjugation by its local image preserve the local boxes of $R_1$, respectively `meetOrder R₁ (sf₂ ℓ)`, and of $\Lambda_1$ at each place other than $v_2$. The map $\mathtt{dIso}_2$ sends an element $\tau$ of the decomposition subgroup of $A_2$ over $\mathbb Q$ to an isometric automorphism of $C$ whose underlying ring equivalence acts as $\tau$ (`hdIso₂`). The character $\chi$ takes values in $\mathbb Z/2$ (multiplicatively written) and is required to be trivial on inertia (`hχ₁`), non-trivial at any Frobenius at $q$ (`hχ₂`), and trivial exactly on those $\tau$ fixing all elements $x$ of the residue field of $A_2$ with $x^{q^2} = x$ (`hχ₃`).
--
--   **Coefficient rings.** $\mathcal O_0$ is a discrete valuation ring of characteristic zero, an integral domain, with irreducible element $\pi$, $\pi$-adically complete, of residue cardinality $q$, with $(q) = (\pi)$, and $F_2$ is its field of fractions; moreover $N$ is invertible in $\mathcal O_0$ (`hN₀`), $\mathcal O_0$ is exactly the set of elements of $F_2$ of valuation at most $1$ in $C$ (`h𝒪₀`), and $\pi$ maps to $\varpi_2$ (`hπϖ`). $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ (`Onr`) is an $\mathcal O_0$-algebra, a domain of characteristic zero, equipped with an $\mathcal O_0$-algebra automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$, complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$, with that ideal maximal (`hOm`), whose residue ring is algebraic over that of $\mathcal O_0$ (`hOa`) and algebraically closed in the sense that every monic polynomial of positive degree has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOcl`), and with $\mathrm{Fr}(x) \equiv x^{q}$ modulo $\pi$ (`hFr`). The homomorphism $\mathtt{vdet}$ from $\mathrm{GL}_2(F_2)$ to $\mathbb Z$ (multiplicatively written) is characterised by `hvdet`: $\mathtt{vdet}(g) = n$ precisely when $\det g$ is a unit of $\mathcal O_0$ times $\pi^{n}$. A ring map $\psi_0 : \mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to C$ is given with values of valuation at most $1$ (`hψ₀`) and agreeing with the inclusion of $\mathcal O_0$ on $\mathcal O_0$ (`hψ₀𝒪`), and `hrk` asserts that for $x$ of valuation $<1$ and $y \neq 0$ in $C$ some power $v(x)^n$ is at most $v(y)$.
--
--   **The integral models and their Hecke and Atkin–Lehner structure.** $f_0 : \mathcal X_0 \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O_0$ with the moduli assignment $\mathtt{pt}_0$ is a coarse moduli scheme for fake elliptic curves for $(\Lambda, N)$ in the full sense of `IsCoarseModuli` (the four clauses above together with the universal property), and $\mathcal X_0$ is integral and $f_0$ flat, separated, locally of finite type and quasi-compact. For each prime $\ell \notin \{q,q'\}$, $g_0(\ell) : \mathcal Y_0(\ell) \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O_0$ with $\mathtt{ptT}_0(\ell)$ is a coarse moduli scheme for such curves with an extra $\ell$-level structure. Morphisms $d_{0}(\ell), d_{1}(\ell) : \mathcal Y_0(\ell) \to \mathcal X_0$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O_0$ (`hd₀f`, `hd₁f`) are the two degeneracy maps: $d_0$ forgets the extra level (`hd₀`) and $d_1$ passes to the target of an $\ell$-isogeny (`hd₁`). Morphisms $\mathrm{ar}$ and $\overline{\mathrm{ar}}$ of $\mathcal X_0$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O_0$ (`harf`, `harbarf`) realise the Atkin–Lehner quotients at $q$ and at $q'$ on moduli points (`har`, `harbar`).
--
--   **The Drinfeld uniformisation data.** $\Theta$ and $\Theta_T(\ell)$ assign, to each $\mathcal O_0$-algebra $B$ in which $\pi$ is nilpotent and each pair consisting of an $\mathcal O_0$-algebra map $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to B$ and a Deligne datum over $B$ for $(F_2, \pi)$, a $B$-point of $\mathcal X_0$, respectively of $\mathcal Y_0(\ell)$, over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O_0$. The hypothesis `hCD` is a conjunction of ten clauses (their contents summarised here): $\Theta$ and each $\Theta_T(\ell)$ are natural in $B$; $\Theta$ is invariant under the twisted action `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct π Onr Fr vdet` of the matrix image of any $\gamma \in \mathtt{awayUnits}\,R_1\,v_2$, and $\Theta_T(\ell)$ under that of any $\gamma$ in the intersection of this group with its conjugate by $s_2(\ell)$; over an algebraically closed $\mathcal O_0$-field with $\pi$ nilpotent, and for fixed $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$-component, $\Theta$ and $\Theta_T(\ell)$ are surjective onto the points of $\mathcal X_0$, respectively $\mathcal Y_0(\ell)$, and identify exactly the orbits of those twisted actions; $\Theta$ and $\Theta_T(\ell)$ are universal among natural invariant families, so that a natural transformation out of the nilpotent-point functor of $f_0$, respectively $g_0(\ell)$, is determined uniquely by its composite with them; composing $\Theta_T(\ell)$ with $d_0(\ell)$ gives $\Theta$, and composing it with $d_1(\ell)$ gives $\Theta$ evaluated on the $s_2(\ell)$-translate; composing $\Theta$ with $\mathrm{ar}$ twists the $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$-component by $\mathrm{Fr}$ and leaves the Deligne datum unchanged; and composing $\Theta$ with $\overline{\mathrm{ar}}$ corresponds to the twisted action of $\bar w_2(\mathrm{none})$.
--
--   **The comparison over $C$ and the pinning of $j$.** The base changes $\mathcal X_C$ and $\mathcal Y_0(\ell) \times_{\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O_0} \operatorname{Spec} C$ are assumed integral. An isomorphism
--   $$u : X \times_{\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z[1/D]} \operatorname{Spec} C \;\cong\; \mathcal X_C$$
--   is given, where the left-hand fibre product is taken along $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the embedding $\bar{\mathbb Q} \to C$ followed by $\bar s$; it is compatible with the projections to $\operatorname{Spec} C$ (`hu`) and carries the moduli points of `pt` to those of $\mathtt{pt}_0$ for every $C$-algebra base point (`hupt`). Finally $j$ is a ring map from $\bar F$ to the function field of $\mathcal X_C$, pinned by `hpin` as follows: writing $\theta : \mathcal X_C \to \mathfrak M.C$ for the composite of $u^{-1}$, of the canonical morphism to $X \times_{\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z[1/D]} \operatorname{Spec}\bar{\mathbb Q}$ obtained from the two projections, and of $e_{\mathfrak M}^{-1}$, then for every $x \in \bar F$, every open $U \subseteq \mathfrak M.C$ containing the generic point of $\mathfrak M.C$ with the generic point of $\mathcal X_C$ in $\theta^{-1}U$, and every section $\mathrm{sec}$ over $U$ whose germ at the generic point of $\mathfrak M.C$ is $\mathfrak M.\mathtt{ffEquiv}(x)$, the germ at the generic point of $\mathcal X_C$ of the pull-back $(\theta^{\#}_U)(\mathrm{sec})$ is $j(x)$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every $\tau$ in the decomposition subgroup of $A_2$ over $\mathbb Q$ and every endomorphism $t_C$ of $\mathcal X_C$ such that $t_C$ followed by the first projection to $\mathcal X_0$ is the first projection, and $t_C$ followed by the second projection to $\operatorname{Spec} C$ is the second projection followed by $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the ring automorphism underlying $\mathtt{dIso}_2(\tau)$, the following holds: for every $x \in \bar F$, every open $U \subseteq \mathcal X_C$ containing the generic point of $\mathcal X_C$, with the generic point also lying in $t_C^{-1}U$, and every section $\mathrm{sec}$ of the structure sheaf over $U$ whose germ at the generic point of $\mathcal X_C$ equals $j(x)$, the germ at the generic point of $\mathcal X_C$, taken on $t_C^{-1}U$, of the pull-back $(t_C^{\#}{}_U)(\mathrm{sec})$ equals $j\bigl(\mathtt{gal}(\tau) \cdot x\bigr)$, where $\mathtt{gal}(\tau)$ denotes the semilinear automorphism of $\bar F$ attached by `gal` to the field automorphism of $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ underlying $\tau$, acting on $x$.
--
--   This is the equivariance clause, for the decomposition group at the place above $q$, of the comparison between the Čerednik–Drinfeld integral model of the Shimura curve and its curve model over $\bar{\mathbb Q}$: the function-field embedding $j$ pinned by the comparison isomorphism transforms under a lift of $\tau$ to the base change over the completion by the semilinear Galois action on $\bar F$. It is used by [`CerednikDrinfeld.exists_ringHom_functionField_of_iso_pullback_completion_zero_one_of_two_mul_dvd`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.exists_ringHom_functionField_of_iso_pullback_completion_zero_one_of_two_mul_dvd), where the pinned embedding together with its Galois equivariance is produced as a package.
